Chapter: HandlerNestedOps
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Two effect operations of a nested handle, read in one expression.

 n1 and n2 pin the operand save: an operation name is a bare name in the
 source and a call in the machine, so the left operand of `get-a + get-b`
 has to survive the branch that fetches the right. Answering 40 there is
 both operands reading the second call's result. Clause order is varied
 because it must not matter.

 n3 and n4 pin the handler save: a handle saves the handler it displaces,
 and doing that on the stack moves SP out from under every spill slot in
 the body. The multiplier makes the two operations tell each other apart,
 and the two handles in sequence are what makes one overwrite the other.

Section: Effects

  effect A where
    get-a : [A] Integer

  effect B where
    get-b : [B] Integer

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let n1 = with A (with B (get-a + get-b))
      get-b (resume) = resume 20
      get-a (resume) = resume 22
    in let n2 = with A (with B (get-a + get-b))
      get-a (resume) = resume 22
      get-b (resume) = resume 20
    in let n3 = with A (with B (get-a * 100 + get-b))
      get-b (resume) = resume 20
      get-a (resume) = resume 22
    in let n4 = with A (with B (get-b * 100 + get-a))
      get-b (resume) = resume 20
      get-a (resume) = resume 22
    in act
      print-line-uni ("n1: " & show n1)
      print-line-uni ("n2: " & show n2)
      print-line-uni ("n3: " & show n3)
      print-line-uni ("n4: " & show n4)
    end

